  - "https://yeetz.dev/bedrock/vertex/decision-0004-streaming-value-io-v2"
statement: >
  Accepted 2026-08-23 by human merge of PR #33 (merge commit
  60cb5c35) after a two-round independent adversarial review — both
  rounds returned AMEND (round two: G1-G5 plus five nitpicks) and
  landed as forward commits on the proposal branch; the final
  re-check at witness tip b3c13365 raised zero blocking findings.
  Chosen: additive AtomicKeyspace::delete_objects over S3
  DeleteObjects beside the published delete_many — neither wraps the
  other; delete_many stays frozen (A6/G117) and not deprecated, and
  two response-trust implementations deliberately coexist (the
  legacy object_store path keeps its missing-member-defaults-to-
  success and unrequested-key panic residuals). Transport: sequential
  chunks of at most 1,000 keys under DELETE_OBJECTS_MAX_INPUT =
  100_000, verbose responses only, exact per-chunk response
  bijection — no omitted key ever defaults to success; batch is
  transport, never transaction, and cross-key atomicity is a
  permanent non-goal. No batched conditional delete: delete_if_match
  remains the only conditional surface, per-key, permanently.
  Outcomes: one typed result per input key — Ok, Rejected,
  Unconfirmed, NotAttempted, Unsupported — behind an admission-only
  outer error; an Unconfirmed stop labels its untouched tail
  NotAttempted, a definitive mid-call refusal labels current chunk
  and tail Unsupported, and remaining() is checkpoint serialization,
  never retry policy. The primitive sits below lifecycle machinery:
  no tombstone, no incarnation bump, no fence read; its
  fence-blindness couples to ADR 0004 quiescence as the named
  residual — raw batch delete plus same-incarnation/version-0
  recreate can re-materialize the exact chunk paths a concurrent
  sweep classified as orphaned, producing ADR 0004's forbidden
  ManifestIncomplete (witnessed as KeyspaceError::ChunkMissing; up
  to 1,000 controls per request). Claims A36-A45; a real-S3
  DeleteObjects leg gates publication; implementation is one
  separately authorized kernel-extension batch targeting the next
  free 0.4.x. Rejected: mutating delete_many in place, wrapping
  delete_many over the new method, object_store::delete_stream for
  the assured path, one top-level wholesale error, read-then-delete,
  rollback/compensation (permanent). Flips if: a portable
  standards-level multi-object conditional (a per-object If-Match
  equivalent) emerges — that demands a superseding ADR with backend
  qualification, never a silent extension; cross-key
  transactionality stays out regardless.
